.TH UNAME 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
uname \- print system information
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uname
.RB [ \-amnrspvio ]
.br
.B uname \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uname
prints one line on standard output that describes the operating system
and the machine it runs on.
Each option selects a field.
The line holds the selected fields in a fixed order, whatever the order
of the options: kernel name
.RB ( \-s ),
host name
.RB ( \-n ),
release
.RB ( \-r ),
version
.RB ( \-v ),
machine
.RB ( \-m ),
processor
.RB ( \-p ),
hardware platform
.RB ( \-i ),
and OS name
.RB ( \-o ).
A single space separates the fields, and a newline ends the line.
.PP
With no options,
.B uname
prints the kernel name, as
.B \-s
does.
The fields describe Windows, not a Unix kernel; each option below gives
its value.
.SH OPTIONS
Short options can be grouped, as in
.BR \-sr .
Each long option is a synonym for a short one, and may be abbreviated to
any unique prefix, so
.B \-\-mach
means
.BR \-\-machine .
A prefix that fits several options, such as
.BR \-\-kernel ,
is an error.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Repeating an option has no effect.
.B uname
takes no operands, and an operand is an error.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Print every field except the processor and hardware platform, whose
values are unknown.
Other field options given with
.B \-a
have no effect.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-machine
Print the processor architecture that busybox runs as:
.B x86_64
in the 64-bit kit and
.B i686
in the 32-bit kit; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nodename
Print the host name, as the Winsock
.B gethostname
function reports it.
If the name cannot be read, this prints
.BR unknown .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-kernel\-release ", " \-\-release
Print the Windows version as the major and minor numbers separated by a
period, such as
.B 5.1
on Windows XP,
.B 6.1
on Windows 7, and
.B 10.0
on Windows 10 and 11.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-kernel\-name
Print the kernel name, which is always
.BR Windows_NT .
This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-processor
Print the processor type, which is always
.BR unknown .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-kernel\-version
Print the Windows build number in decimal, such as
.BR 26200 .
Windows 11 builds start at 22000.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-hardware\-platform
Print the hardware platform, which is always
.BR unknown .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-operating\-system
Print the operating system name, which is always
.BR MS/Windows .
.TP
.B \-\-help
When it is the first argument, print a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0, ignoring the other arguments.
In any other position it is an unknown option.
It cannot be abbreviated:
.B \-\-h
means
.BR \-\-hardware\-platform .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B uname
reads no environment variables of its own.
The variables that busybox reads for every applet, such as
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown or ambiguous option, an argument given to a long option, an
operand, or an error writing standard output.
For all but the write error, a usage message is written to standard
error, and for an option error a line naming the option comes first.
A write error is reported on standard error without a usage message.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the kernel name and the architecture:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uname \-sm
Windows_NT x86_64
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the Windows version and build number:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uname \-rv
10.0 26200
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test for a build number of Windows 11 or later:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ [ "$(uname \-v)" \-ge 22000 ] && echo Windows 11
Windows 11
.EE
.RE
.PP
Name a build directory after the architecture, with a long option:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir \-p "build/$(uname \-\-machine)"
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Unlike GNU
.BR uname ,
it has no
.B \-\-version
option, and its
.B \-\-help
works only as the first argument.
.PP
The values come from the Windows API.
The release and version are those of
.BR GetVersionEx ,
and the machine comes from
.BR GetSystemInfo .
Windows 10 and Windows 11 both have release 10.0; the build number
tells them apart.
Windows reports the true version because the manifest of
.I busybox.exe
declares support for Windows 10.
.PP
.B GetSystemInfo
reports the architecture the process runs as, not that of the machine.
So on 64-bit Windows the 32-bit kit, which runs under WOW64, prints
.BR i686 ,
and on ARM64 Windows the 64-bit kit, which runs under x64 emulation,
prints
.BR x86_64 .
.PP
The kit's busybox has no
.B hostname
applet;
.B "uname \-n"
prints the host name.
.PP
The kit's
.B arch
is the same as
.BR "uname \-m" ;
see
.BR arch (1).
.PP
The output line ends with LF, not CRLF.
.PP
The kit's
.B sh
runs
.B uname
inside the shell process, without starting a new one, except where the
shell needs a separate process anyway, as for a command substitution or
a pipeline.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR arch (1),
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1)
